  "result": "The 30-minute lesson, \"Context and memory: what the assistant sees, keeps and looks up\", is written. A short quiz on it is ready when you want it.\n\nBy the end you can decide, for each fact in a makerspace request, where it belongs:\n- **In the context:** the model keeps nothing between calls, so your program resends the whole message list each step. That makes input cost grow roughly with the square of the step count.\n- **In saved notes:** short notes carry preferences between sessions. Whether a student may use a machine comes from the training record, read through a tool at that moment. A note or the student's claim never decides it.\n- **In retrieval:** only the relevant chunks of a long manual go into the window. The lesson includes a cosine-similarity example with numbers and code.\n\nThe lesson also covers tokens, the context window, context rot, clearing and compaction. It has worked examples for cost, compaction and sorting facts, plus a budget practice problem.\n\nI checked the lesson against the two sources, Anthropic's context-engineering post and the Claude context-window documentation, using review agents. A page-length check I tried to run was blocked, so the word count is the reviewer's estimate of about 4,350 words, which is within the 30-minute range.\n\nAsk me about any part you want explained again.\n\n**Files are in `C:\\Users\\gunna\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\p3-d6i9bt3e`:**\n- lessons/0001-context-and-memory.html\n- quizzes/0002.json\n- keys/0002.json\n- cheatsheets/context-and-memory.html\n- review-records/0001-lesson.md\n- RESOURCES.md\n- NOTES.md (updated)\n\nYour quiz 0001 attempts, `s1` and `s2`, are still ungraded. Do you want them graded next?",
